  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 1215 AM Saturday... \n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... A pair of low pressure systems will track northeast \nalong coastal areas this weekend, bringing a good chance of rain but \nstill with considerable uncertainty in exact amounts.\n\nA shortwave crossing northern Florida is interacting with an east-\nwest oriented stationary boundary, with multiple waves of low \npressure evident along the front. Based on surface observations and \nsatellite imagery, there appears to be a broad area of low pressure \nover northeast Florida and a second area further east over the \nAtlantic where there is disorganized convection.\n\nModel guidance seems to have locked in on the low over Florida \ndrifting north, but weakening as it moves along the Georgia/South \nCarolina coastline. Meanwhile, the low further out over the Atlantic \nmoves north and becomes the dominant feature, tracking near the \nOuter Banks tonight and along the Mid-Atlantic coast Sunday and \nMonday. This scenario has been supported by several model runs, but \nhighly variable low tracks lead to continued uncertainty in rainfall \ntotals. \n\nModels show mixed signals regarding how much rain North \nCarolina will receive over the next 36 hours. There are signs of a \nrelative QPF minimum over central NC, sandwiched between rain moving \ninto the mountains and higher amounts associated with the primary \nlow along the coast. However, model variance remains high less than \n24 hours out; for example, the ECMWF suggests up to an inch in the \nwestern Piedmont with lesser amounts in the Coastal Plain, while the \n18Z GFS trended toward a nearly dry solution over central NC. The \nRRFS and HRRR also suggest a weakening trend in precipitation \nassociated with the low moving up the GA/SC coast, resulting in \nlower QPF overall.\n\nSo while the official forecast is relatively consistent with \nprevious forecasts, the wide range of ensemble guidance still \nsuggests a range of very little to 1-2 inches in portions of central \nNC, but with a notable downward trend in forcing for those higher \namounts.\n\nCloud cover and rain into a dry atmosphere with dewpoints in the \nupper 20s and 30s early this morning will eventually lock in a cold \nair damming airmass, and without a stronger low moving through the \nCoastal Plain, chances of any thunderstorms appears very low.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Another round of rain is possible from mid to late \nnext week, along with much cooler temperatures.\n\nIn the wake of the coastal low this weekend, a mostly dry cold front \nis forecast to push south through the area Monday night. A strong \n1035+ mb surface high will follow, building across the Great Lakes \nand extending into NC through Wednesday. This will bring a shot of \ncooler, below-normal temperatures through mid-week before another \ntrough crossing the central US brings potential for a low to develop \nover the Southeast, increasing rain chances heading into \nThanksgiving weekend. Given that the upper-level energy driving this \ntrough is still over the Pacific, there is plenty of time for the \nforecast to evolve, but models maintain a consistent signal for \nanother chance of much-needed rainfall.",
